course: dictionary
lesson: 14913
title: Redeposit and the prefix re-
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The prefix re- means again. So redeposit means to deposit again: to put something back in or down, often where it was before.
explain: «Sam took £20 out of the bank, then redeposited it the next day.» The clue is re-, which means again, and deposit means to put something in. Sam put the money back into the bank.
explain: In science, rivers carry sand and stones along. «The river carried sand down the valley and redeposited it on the beach.» Here redeposit means the river dropped the sand again in a new place.
fact: Redeposit is a verb: redeposit, redeposits, redeposited, redepositing.
fact: To redeposit money means to put it back into a bank or account.
fact: In science, to redeposit means to drop sand, mud or stones again in a new place.
fact: Phrases you may meet: «redeposit the money» and «redeposit sediment».
fact: A redeposit (noun) is money or material that has been put down again.
exercise: «Sam took out £20 and then redeposited it the next day.» What did Sam do with the money? | *Put it back into the bank | Spent it all on sweets | Took it out again | Gave it to a friend
exercise: «The flood carried mud down the river and redeposited it on the fields.» Where did the mud go? | Into the sea only | *On the fields, in a new place | Back up into the clouds | Up the hill
exercise: In redeposit, what does the prefix re- mean? | Under | *Again | Never | Before
test: Which word means to put material down again? | Withdraw | *Redeposit | Carry | Deposit
test: What is a redeposit? | *Money or material put down again | A type of rock | A holiday by the sea | A map of the river
exam: «The waves picked up the sand and redeposited it further along the coast.» What does redeposited mean here? | *Dropped again in a new place | Frozen | Washed away for ever | Dug up
